WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.260
- Nuclear power, exactly what is it?

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.060
What is happening inside of that reactor

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.941
aside from making superheros?

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:11.524
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.500
- We learned in the 19th century

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.740
that there's certain elements
that we dig out of the ground

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:23.670
that are warm when there's no
other source of heat going on.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.000
So we said hmm, what's going on there?

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.340
And through the works of Marie Curie

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.370
and other sort of early, Henri Becquerel,

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:36.470
and there's some physicists way back then

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.710
who were the leaders
in trying to figure out

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:43.150
what these properties are.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.350
And then ended up
calling it radioactivity.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.470
- Radioactivity?
- Right.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.670
And so when something's radioactive, ooh,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.460
what it means is there
are unstable elements

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.750
in that substance.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:56.770
If it's unstable,

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.500
it means they're actively
becoming something else.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.710
If you have a heavy element

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.670
and it naturally then becomes
a slightly lighter element,

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.670
it will give off energy.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:07.550
- Energy.
- Yeah.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.780
And it'll manifest as heat in that object.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.613
- Okay.

00:01:10.613 --> 00:01:13.520
- Turn of the century,
like 1800s to 1900s,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.650
people started wondering,
well, how much energy is it?

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.130
Then Einstein writes
down E equals MC squared,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.300
equating mass and energy.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.010
So if you're getting
energy out of the atom,

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.300
where's it coming from?

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.763
It must be losing mass.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.960
We were pretty sure you can
get energy out of the atom,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.070
but we had to sort of
develop the physics of that.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.870
And quantum physics had to come forward.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.880
That was in the 1920s.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.593
Quantum physics is the
physics of the small,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.960
atoms, molecules, that sort of thing.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.097
Once that was put into place, oh my gosh.

00:01:48.097 --> 00:01:49.452
- We got something.
- We got something here.

00:01:49.452 --> 00:01:51.040
- Got something.
- We got something here.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.300
And what do you find out?

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.090
For example, uranium.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.010
- Okay.
- Okay?

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.000
You can send a neutron, what's
the charge on a neutron?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.640
- Should be nothing, right?

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:02.520
- Nothing.
- Right.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.150
- Where'd you get the bar joke, okay?

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:05.983
- What's the bar joke?

00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:08.150
- Neutron goes up to
the bar, orders a drink.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:09.540
Neutron says how much?

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.852
And the bartender says for you, no charge.

00:02:11.852 --> 00:02:13.060
(laughs)

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.240
You didn't know? (laughs)

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.930
What we were deducing
through our equations

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.560
and through experiments was that

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.470
there's this uranium nucleus.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.950
If you hit it with a neutron,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.750
and how can you just
send a neutron in there?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.380
It's got no charge.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.940
Neutrons go right through
the front door of anything.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.110
There's no electrical
repulsion or attraction to it.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.030
It'll just, I want to go here?

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:40.900
I'll go there.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.060
I want to go here, I'll go there.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.190
It marches through the front door

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.614
of the nucleus of the atom.

00:02:45.614 --> 00:02:46.990
- All access pass, baby.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.068
- All access backstage!
- I'm a neutron!

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.960
- I come in the front,
back, side, top, or bottom.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.000
- Any door you want.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.010
- Okay, so it comes in.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:54.980
If it has enough energy,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.980
it can break the uranium
atom nucleus into two pieces.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.240
And we're pretty sure we knew at the time

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.500
what two pieces it would be.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.420
It would be two other elements,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.893
and it would release two neutrons.

00:03:09.926 --> 00:03:13.240
- [Chuck] Oh, it would have the ability

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.550
to go through any other door they want.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:16.970
- And if you have a--
- And then they

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.350
tell two friends--
- And then they tell--

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.995
- [Chuck] And then they tell two friends.

00:03:19.995 --> 00:03:20.830
- Yes, yes.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.820
Then you have a runaway process.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.063
- Man, we got ourselves a reaction!

00:03:25.063 --> 00:03:26.210
- A reaction!

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.805
Okay, a chain reaction.
- A chain!

00:03:27.805 --> 00:03:29.190
- Remember, we haven't heart
that expression in a while.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:30.023
A chain reaction.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:30.023 --> 00:03:33.120
- So what you need is
enough sort of critical mass

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.950
of uranium so that when the neutrons go in

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.300
and they tell two friends
and they tell two friends,

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.480
that you get enough neutrons

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.078
so that every single uranium
atom by the end of that

00:03:43.078 --> 00:03:44.410
gets broken apart.
- Gets broken apart.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.243
- Right.

00:03:45.243 --> 00:03:49.130
Okay, and this is nuclear
fission, big atoms

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.720
becoming little atoms.
- Breaking apart.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.210
So that must produce a tremendous amount

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.470
of energy?
- Oh, yes!

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.670
Because at the end, you have less mass

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:57.503
than when you started,

00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.847
and the act of splitting
an atom releases energy

00:04:00.847 --> 00:04:03.000
and the two pieces have less mass

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.400
than the one piece that you started with.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.910
- So that energy has to go somewhere.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.470
- And so now you build a device

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:10.070
that concentrates that energy

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.740
and you put it in a delivery vessel,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:16.540
and you have what the
military would call a bomb.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.730
That's how every nuclear power
plant is making energy today,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.620
through nuclear fission, okay?

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:23.730
- Which is breaking apart,

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.265
becoming smaller, releasing energy.

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.340
- Right.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.460
- [Narrator] In nuclear power plants,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.180
the energy released by fission heats up

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.760
the water surrounding the reactor.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.130
This turns the water into steam

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.670
which passes through turbines
and spins a generator.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.480
But if the reaction's
not closely regulated,

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.120
it can overheat leading to a meltdown,

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.970
just like what we saw at Fukushima.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.260
So how do the control rods
slow down the reaction?

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.030
By absorbing some of the neutrons.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.280
The rods are composed of elements,

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:46.970
like boron, silver, and cadmium,

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:49.280
that can absorb the neutrons
without undergoing fission.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.230
If the neutrons have an all-access pass

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:52.640
that leads to a chain reaction,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.200
well, think of the
control rod as a bouncer.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.610
Without regulating this chain
reaction with control rods,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.430
the temperature would keep increasing.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.230
With them, we can keep
the fission reaction

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.063
at a temperature we can control.

00:05:02.063 --> 00:05:03.900
- And so this is the fear factor

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.830
that people have of nuclear power plants.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:06.663
- Not in my backyard!

00:05:06.663 --> 00:05:10.380
- You don't want to put it on a fault line

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:11.310
or where they're think,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.170
you want to sort of protect these things.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:14.410
- Gotcha.
- Right, right.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.130
You can break atoms apart only so far

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.540
and expect to get energy out of it.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.210
- Okay.
- Okay, now put a pin in that.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:27.180
At the other end, you have
hydrogen, the lightest element.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.870
You can fuse hydrogen atoms together

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.918
and get heavier elements.

00:05:31.918 --> 00:05:34.257
And that takes heat, so
thermonuclear fusion.

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:36.902
- Thermonuclear fusion.

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:37.735
- Yes.

00:05:37.735 --> 00:05:40.180
So you can bring hydrogen
atoms together and make helium.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.000
Bring helium together, make carbon.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.300
Nitrogen, oxygen, silicon.

00:05:44.267 --> 00:05:46.950
Each of those releases energy, as well.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.430
- [Narrator] The nuclei
of atoms are held together

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:50.760
by the strong force which attracts protons

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.540
and neutrons together strongly

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.270
but only when they get
really close together.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.700
After the width of a few protons,

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.200
the attraction falls to zero.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.330
Without the strong force,

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.450
all the protons packed
into one small space

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.090
would repel each other and the
stable nuclei can never form.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.410
And as they fuse to form
larger and larger elements

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.150
with more protons and neutrons,

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.950
their nuclei are more stable
since they're bound together

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.210
by more and more of the strong force.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.500
Fusing light elements together
makes more stable atoms,

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.950
releasing leftover energy,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:17.130
but as the elements get heavier,

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.570
each additional proton
fuels a repulsive force

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.520
from any of the protons
that aren't super close.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.700
And as we get heavier than iron,

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.270
the nuclei become less stable.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.450
And when they get really big,

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:26.840
the tap from a neutron is all they need

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.320
to break apart into two
smaller more stable elements,

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.450
releasing a whole lot of
energy in the process.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.210
- Seems like no matter
what you do with an atom

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.090
you're going end up with some energy.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.060
- Okay, so turns out if you fuse iron,

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.453
it sucks energy out of you.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:40.681
- So it defeats the purpose.

00:06:40.681 --> 00:06:41.514
- Defeats the purpose.

00:06:41.514 --> 00:06:42.770
- Right, gotcha.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:47.770
- Stars undergo fusion, and
if you have enough mass,

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.620
it'll go hydrogen, helium,
and it'll do that in its core.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.860
And it reaches, a star's in
the business of making energy.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.960
That's all they know how to
do, and that's all they do.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:57.880
They get to iron in the core,

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.510
they try to fuse it, it sucks energy.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:00.930
They try to fuse it, it sucks energy.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.440
- Before you know it,
that star is just like

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:03.630
what's happening to me?

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.651
All my energy seems to be
going back in on myself.

00:07:05.651 --> 00:07:06.957
- And then it collapses

00:07:06.957 --> 00:07:11.020
